1 STOP
    In a command procedure, or after CTRL/Y has interrupted one, STOP
    with no process ends every command procedure, returning to the
    terminal (command level 0); $STATUS is left as it was.  ON ERROR THEN STOP in a nested procedure therefore
    doesn't return to the procedure that ran it.  At the terminal, STOP
    with no process ends an image CTRL/C or CTRL/Y left stopped.

    With a process, the STOP command deletes it, and the subprocesses it created
    before it.  The process is named as SHOW PROCESS names one.  Its files
    are closed, its termination mailbox (if it had one) is sent the
    termination message, with the final status %SYSTEM-F-ABORT, and a
    process suspended is resumed first so that it can end.  Process 1, the
    console's own process, can't be stopped; EXIT ends the session.

    STOP/IMAGE with a process ends the image it runs, but not the process:
    the image exits as $FORCEX makes it, its exit handlers called, with
    the status %SYSTEM-F-FORCEDEXIT, and a subprocess's DCL goes on with
    its next command.  Without a process, STOP/IMAGE is STOP.

    /EXIT=USER_MODE runs the process's image's exit handlers before it is
    deleted: the image is made to exit, as with /IMAGE, and the process
    is deleted when it has.  govax's images have exit handlers only in
    user mode, so /EXIT's other modes (EXECUTIVE_MODE, the default) and
    /NOEXIT delete the process at once.

    INITIALIZE, VMINIT, ZERO, and leaving govax delete every process but
    process 1 in the same way.

    Syntax:

        STOP [process-name] [/IDENTIFICATION=pid] [/IMAGE]
             [/EXIT[=mode] | /NOEXIT]

    Parameter:

        process-name    The name of the process, as SHOW SYSTEM lists
                        it (quote a name with blanks or lowercase).

    Qualifier:

        /IDENTIFICATION=pid
                        The process ID, in hexadecimal, as SHOW SYSTEM
                        lists it.
        /IMAGE          End the process's image, not the process.
        /EXIT[=mode]    The exit handlers to run before the deletion:
                        those of mode and the more privileged modes
                        (KERNEL_MODE, EXECUTIVE_MODE, the default,
                        SUPERVISOR_MODE, USER_MODE).
        /NOEXIT         Run no exit handlers.

    Examples:

        STOP/IDENTIFICATION=00000305
        STOP/IMAGE LIBRA
        STOP/EXIT=USER_MODE LIBRA
